1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,970
In this video, I'll continue
the discussion about

2
00:00:02,970 --> 00:00:06,240
embedded testing and talk

3
00:00:06,240 --> 00:00:08,715
specifically about
automated testing.

4
00:00:08,715 --> 00:00:10,890
We want to understand
the components of

5
00:00:10,890 --> 00:00:14,040
an automated test
framework, introduce mocks,

6
00:00:14,040 --> 00:00:16,710
and introduce the unity
and GoogleTest as

7
00:00:16,710 --> 00:00:20,230
two possible automated
test frameworks.

8
00:00:20,510 --> 00:00:25,185
The components of an
automated test setup.

9
00:00:25,185 --> 00:00:27,030
The first one is the framework.

10
00:00:27,030 --> 00:00:28,950
What do I mean by
a test framework?

11
00:00:28,950 --> 00:00:31,800
I mean it's something that's
able to create executables

12
00:00:31,800 --> 00:00:35,355
on whatever platform you
plan to run the tests on.

13
00:00:35,355 --> 00:00:38,765
It often includes
features like grouping

14
00:00:38,765 --> 00:00:42,770
tests by whatever feature
the specific test covers,

15
00:00:42,770 --> 00:00:45,380
selecting specific tests to run,

16
00:00:45,380 --> 00:00:47,660
maybe in allowing
for selection of

17
00:00:47,660 --> 00:00:52,040
specific tests and
reporting status back about

18
00:00:52,040 --> 00:00:55,670
which steps failed and some
detail about what failed so

19
00:00:55,670 --> 00:00:57,350
that it's easy to go and find

20
00:00:57,350 --> 00:00:59,870
the problem and fix
it when it happens.

21
00:00:59,870 --> 00:01:01,520
A lot of times

22
00:01:01,520 --> 00:01:03,485
the test framework will
be set up such that

23
00:01:03,485 --> 00:01:06,320
the executables and
the testing that you

24
00:01:06,320 --> 00:01:07,910
perform is going to be run on

25
00:01:07,910 --> 00:01:10,430
a development platform instead
of the target platform.

26
00:01:10,430 --> 00:01:14,270
That's a lot of just
convenience so that you

27
00:01:14,270 --> 00:01:15,980
maybe don't have the
extra interaction

28
00:01:15,980 --> 00:01:17,090
required with setting up your

29
00:01:17,090 --> 00:01:18,695
test on the
development platform.

30
00:01:18,695 --> 00:01:20,540
Similar to what we've done with

31
00:01:20,540 --> 00:01:22,760
the assignments this
semester where we start out

32
00:01:22,760 --> 00:01:25,370
implementing our tests on

33
00:01:25,370 --> 00:01:29,740
native Ubuntu system
for instance.

34
00:01:29,740 --> 00:01:33,110
Then a second test component

35
00:01:33,110 --> 00:01:35,900
from automated test
is test mocks.

36
00:01:35,900 --> 00:01:39,235
I'll talk more about
these in the next slides.

37
00:01:39,235 --> 00:01:42,920
A test mock can simulate
the behavior of

38
00:01:42,920 --> 00:01:44,870
a real object in controlled
ways that you can

39
00:01:44,870 --> 00:01:47,840
control in your test definition.

40
00:01:47,840 --> 00:01:50,900
A mock is really important
and necessary when you have

41
00:01:50,900 --> 00:01:54,735
things like
non-deterministic results.

42
00:01:54,735 --> 00:01:56,420
For instance, if you have
code that's trying to

43
00:01:56,420 --> 00:01:58,880
read current time or
current temperature,

44
00:01:58,880 --> 00:02:00,230
that's going to vary every

45
00:02:00,230 --> 00:02:01,670
single time you
run the test case.

46
00:02:01,670 --> 00:02:04,640
It'll be difficult to get
deterministic results

47
00:02:04,640 --> 00:02:06,050
where something always passes or

48
00:02:06,050 --> 00:02:08,140
fails in specific scenarios.

49
00:02:08,140 --> 00:02:10,865
Same with states that are
difficult to reproduce.

50
00:02:10,865 --> 00:02:12,950
Let's say you've got
some handling for

51
00:02:12,950 --> 00:02:15,410
the case where a device
returns an error condition.

52
00:02:15,410 --> 00:02:16,670
Well, how do you make the device

53
00:02:16,670 --> 00:02:17,780
return an error condition

54
00:02:17,780 --> 00:02:20,945
depending on what you're
doing for an error condition,

55
00:02:20,945 --> 00:02:22,130
it might be difficult to do

56
00:02:22,130 --> 00:02:23,945
that from a device perspective.

57
00:02:23,945 --> 00:02:26,675
But a mock can help you
simulate that case.

58
00:02:26,675 --> 00:02:29,185
We'll talk about
that in a minute.

59
00:02:29,185 --> 00:02:32,325
Other cases where mocks
are useful are when

60
00:02:32,325 --> 00:02:37,650
the interaction is either
slow or requires setup.

61
00:02:37,650 --> 00:02:39,290
You can think about
an interaction

62
00:02:39,290 --> 00:02:40,805
with a database, for instance,

63
00:02:40,805 --> 00:02:44,600
that in order to have
some specific state,

64
00:02:44,600 --> 00:02:46,745
you might need to
set up a database

65
00:02:46,745 --> 00:02:49,580
and get that database
in some specific state.

66
00:02:49,580 --> 00:02:52,040
The procedure for doing
that might be complicated,

67
00:02:52,040 --> 00:02:53,240
might take a lot of work to do,

68
00:02:53,240 --> 00:02:55,350
so simulating the database

69
00:02:55,350 --> 00:02:58,595
instead might be a faster
option through a mock.

70
00:02:58,595 --> 00:03:00,770
The other cases would be that

71
00:03:00,770 --> 00:03:02,330
the thing you're interacting

72
00:03:02,330 --> 00:03:03,760
with doesn't actually exist yet.

73
00:03:03,760 --> 00:03:05,345
Often it's common that

74
00:03:05,345 --> 00:03:07,040
two different development
teams will be

75
00:03:07,040 --> 00:03:09,290
working on developing
something for a product.

76
00:03:09,290 --> 00:03:11,570
Neither one of the features
will be complete yet,

77
00:03:11,570 --> 00:03:13,384
you can use a mock to simulate

78
00:03:13,384 --> 00:03:17,180
the feature that's in
development from another test or

79
00:03:17,180 --> 00:03:18,860
another development group
while you're developing

80
00:03:18,860 --> 00:03:22,400
your feature or in the case that

81
00:03:22,400 --> 00:03:25,115
you're modeling what
it's going to look like

82
00:03:25,115 --> 00:03:26,960
if a feature changes

83
00:03:26,960 --> 00:03:29,180
behavior from the way
it is currently now.

84
00:03:29,180 --> 00:03:30,650
Again, maybe that's
something that's in

85
00:03:30,650 --> 00:03:33,040
development on a different
development team.

86
00:03:33,040 --> 00:03:35,330
It's also really
useful for testing

87
00:03:35,330 --> 00:03:38,765
software components and
isolating dependency.

88
00:03:38,765 --> 00:03:41,120
If you're seeing a bug
that's happening in

89
00:03:41,120 --> 00:03:42,230
some interaction between

90
00:03:42,230 --> 00:03:43,715
two different
software components.

91
00:03:43,715 --> 00:03:45,875
Having a mock helps
you prove that

92
00:03:45,875 --> 00:03:48,260
if I try to mock
this same scenario,

93
00:03:48,260 --> 00:03:49,820
I either can or can't reproduce

94
00:03:49,820 --> 00:03:51,875
this with my component
in isolation.

95
00:03:51,875 --> 00:03:53,480
I can give you a
lot of clues about

96
00:03:53,480 --> 00:03:56,190
where the problem
ultimately lies.

97
00:03:57,200 --> 00:04:00,070
I'm going to go through
some examples of how you

98
00:04:00,070 --> 00:04:02,470
can use automated testing,

99
00:04:02,470 --> 00:04:04,945
specifically with
the unity framework.

100
00:04:04,945 --> 00:04:06,490
This is all going to reference

101
00:04:06,490 --> 00:04:10,000
a project that's linked
here in the bottom of

102
00:04:10,000 --> 00:04:12,310
the slide that goes
through some content

103
00:04:12,310 --> 00:04:15,800
from a previous lecture
about time functions.

104
00:04:15,800 --> 00:04:18,550
First, in order to do
automated testing,

105
00:04:18,550 --> 00:04:19,780
you got to start with something

106
00:04:19,780 --> 00:04:22,370
that's interesting to test.

107
00:04:22,370 --> 00:04:24,245
What I'm starting
with, in this case,

108
00:04:24,245 --> 00:04:25,675
is a time spec diff,

109
00:04:25,675 --> 00:04:27,760
that's just going
to return a result

110
00:04:27,760 --> 00:04:29,995
that's the difference
