131
00:05:19,040 --> 00:05:22,870
0.1 for the time
spec difference.

132
00:05:22,870 --> 00:05:25,575
You can see that part
of the assertion.

133
00:05:25,575 --> 00:05:27,200
I can provide a
message here that

134
00:05:27,200 --> 00:05:30,215
says what I'm expecting in Y.

135
00:05:30,215 --> 00:05:31,985
If the test fails,

136
00:05:31,985 --> 00:05:33,620
this assertion is
printed and I'll

137
00:05:33,620 --> 00:05:36,330
have more information
about what failed.

138
00:05:37,070 --> 00:05:39,455
Then the next step,

139
00:05:39,455 --> 00:05:40,550
once you have the test case to

140
00:05:40,550 --> 00:05:42,680
find is you need a
test case runner.

141
00:05:42,680 --> 00:05:44,540
The runner is going
to be the thing that

142
00:05:44,540 --> 00:05:48,080
can actually create the
main entry point for

143
00:05:48,080 --> 00:05:51,170
your executable so
that you can build

144
00:05:51,170 --> 00:05:52,820
this an executable
and it'll find

145
00:05:52,820 --> 00:05:55,720
all the test cases and
run them all for you.

146
00:05:55,720 --> 00:05:57,810
Every test framework is slightly

147
00:05:57,810 --> 00:05:59,235
different in how this works,

148
00:05:59,235 --> 00:06:00,750
for unities there is

149
00:06:00,750 --> 00:06:04,820
a Ruby generate
test runner command

150
00:06:04,820 --> 00:06:06,785
that you can use for this step.

151
00:06:06,785 --> 00:06:09,350
This just requires
you to install Ruby,

152
00:06:09,350 --> 00:06:11,687
which is a one-line apt-get.

153
00:06:11,687 --> 00:06:15,885
I've to get step 4
Ubuntu, for instance.

154
00:06:15,885 --> 00:06:19,140
Then you can run it something
like this where you specify

155
00:06:19,140 --> 00:06:24,195
the content that
you're going to test,

156
00:06:24,195 --> 00:06:26,375
and then the shared
runner that you wanted to

157
00:06:26,375 --> 00:06:28,620
create that pulls
those test functions

158
00:06:28,620 --> 00:06:30,770
out and runs them for you.

159
00:06:31,540 --> 00:06:35,030
Then you can include this all in

160
00:06:35,030 --> 00:06:39,335
a CMake configuration to
create a new project,

161
00:06:39,335 --> 00:06:42,990
and then add the
Unity submodule.

162
00:06:44,080 --> 00:06:46,860
The Unity submodule itself

163
00:06:46,860 --> 00:06:48,575
includes a separate CMake step,

164
00:06:48,575 --> 00:06:50,365
so CMake already will
know how to build

165
00:06:50,365 --> 00:06:53,410
that just from adding
the subdirectory.

166
00:06:53,410 --> 00:06:56,190
Then add all the
include directories so

167
00:06:56,190 --> 00:06:58,350
that reference includes from

168
00:06:58,350 --> 00:07:01,575
Unity for things
like assertions.

169
00:07:01,575 --> 00:07:05,145
Then create that new
executable Unity test here

170
00:07:05,145 --> 00:07:08,345
that will build based
on the C source files,

171
00:07:08,345 --> 00:07:09,900
the test files, and
the test runner

172
00:07:09,900 --> 00:07:11,810
that we generated through
that Ruby script.

173
00:07:11,810 --> 00:07:14,495
Then link it with the
Unity library that gets

174
00:07:14,495 --> 00:07:17,205
built based on adding
