180
00:07:30,840 --> 00:07:32,100
and you've probably
seen this already

181
00:07:32,100 --> 00:07:35,160
from your assignment
implementations.

182
00:07:35,160 --> 00:07:37,770
That covers the basics
of how you can use

183
00:07:37,770 --> 00:07:40,145
Unity with automated testing.

184
00:07:40,145 --> 00:07:44,145
You can also use
CMock as an extension

185
00:07:44,145 --> 00:07:45,880
for Unity that will create

186
00:07:45,880 --> 00:07:48,000
mocks and stubs for
C functions for you.

187
00:07:48,000 --> 00:07:49,815
This is a cool feature of Unity,

188
00:07:49,815 --> 00:07:52,835
specifically, in the way
that they do their mocks.

189
00:07:52,835 --> 00:07:54,600
But what they essentially do,

190
00:07:54,600 --> 00:07:56,550
is create fake versions
of functions that are

191
00:07:56,550 --> 00:07:59,255
outside whatever C
module you're testing.

192
00:07:59,255 --> 00:08:01,500
If you're testing a
specific file and you're

193
00:08:01,500 --> 00:08:04,095
calling another function
that's outside that file,

194
00:08:04,095 --> 00:08:07,295
CMock can create mocks
for you that can

195
00:08:07,295 --> 00:08:12,090
handle the function in
specific ways that you define.

196
00:08:12,090 --> 00:08:14,775
It uses the Ruby scripts to

197
00:08:14,775 --> 00:08:16,620
auto-generate C source that

198
00:08:16,620 --> 00:08:19,275
corresponds to whatever you've
defined in a header file.

199
00:08:19,275 --> 00:08:21,730
For instance, if you
had a file 1.c and

200
00:08:21,730 --> 00:08:23,990
that pound included NeatStuff.h,

201
00:08:23,990 --> 00:08:27,360
and NeatStuff define
these header files.

202
00:08:27,360 --> 00:08:28,590
Then there will be

203
00:08:28,590 --> 00:08:31,650
auto-generated sources
that CMock will create

204
00:08:31,650 --> 00:08:33,760
for you that will
give you a bunch

205
00:08:33,760 --> 00:08:36,555
of ways to interact with
each one of these functions.

206
00:08:36,555 --> 00:08:38,690
Each of these functions
you can call,

207
00:08:38,690 --> 00:08:42,260
will set up how the
mock is going to handle

208
00:08:42,260 --> 00:08:43,940
calls to these functions

209
00:08:43,940 --> 00:08:47,470
from whatever file you're
testing that uses them.

210
00:08:47,780 --> 00:08:51,060
If you're testing
code in a C file,

211
00:08:51,060 --> 00:08:54,060
and that C file pound
includes NeatStuff.h,

212
00:08:54,060 --> 00:08:56,400
and then let's say
it calls ParseStuff,

213
00:08:56,400 --> 00:08:58,365
then instead of calling

214
00:08:58,365 --> 00:09:01,220
the real ParseStuff
in your real file,

215
00:09:01,220 --> 00:09:03,620
it will get redirected
to the mock.

216
00:09:03,620 --> 00:09:05,585
In that case, the test case,

217
00:09:05,585 --> 00:09:07,715
before it calls
whatever function

218
00:09:07,715 --> 00:09:09,530
is going to be
called ParseStuff,

219
00:09:09,530 --> 00:09:12,225
can set up how it wants
the mock to behave.

220
00:09:12,225 --> 00:09:14,760
ExpectAndReturn,
will say that it

221
00:09:14,760 --> 00:09:17,085
expects your code to
pass something in,

222
00:09:17,085 --> 00:09:18,420
and it will simulate

223
00:09:18,420 --> 00:09:20,760
some value to return in
this toReturn value,

224
00:09:20,760 --> 00:09:24,940
so you can set up both how
you expect the interaction to

225
00:09:24,940 --> 00:09:27,255
be with the function as well as

226
00:09:27,255 --> 00:09:30,140
what you want the function
to return back as a mock.

227
00:09:30,140 --> 00:09:32,400
IgnoreAndReturn could
ignore whatever was

228
00:09:32,400 --> 00:09:34,685
passed in and always
return the same value.

229
00:09:34,685 --> 00:09:37,245
Then StubAndCallback could just

230
00:09:37,245 --> 00:09:39,845
take a function pointer
that it calls to do

231
00:09:39,845 --> 00:09:42,360
whatever logic you
wanted custom based on

232
00:09:42,360 --> 00:09:43,775
whenever that code is called

233
00:09:43,775 --> 00:09:46,760
inside your test and
see if function.

234
00:09:47,930 --> 00:09:53,190
We can combine the Unity
implementation with CMock

235
00:09:53,190 --> 00:09:58,790
in another Unity extension
called Ceedling.

236
00:09:58,790 --> 00:10:01,620
Ceedling is a wrapper
around CMock and

237
00:10:01,620 --> 00:10:04,260
Unity and also CException;

238
00:10:04,260 --> 00:10:09,325
a way to do exceptions
in C. It will automate

239
00:10:09,325 --> 00:10:10,800
all the steps associated with

240
00:10:10,800 --> 00:10:12,365
generating those
individual tests files

241
00:10:12,365 --> 00:10:15,455
so that Ruby command that
I mentioned previously.

242
00:10:15,455 --> 00:10:18,010
It also brings in what's
called the Rake framework,

243
00:10:18,010 --> 00:10:20,615
which is a way to automatically

244
00:10:20,615 --> 00:10:23,380
generate dependencies
and build dependencies

245
00:10:23,380 --> 00:10:25,490
based on Ruby files.

246
00:10:25,490 --> 00:10:28,140
There is an example project in

247
00:10:28,140 --> 00:10:30,410
the Ceedling example if
you want to look at it,

248
00:10:30,410 --> 00:10:33,030
but it is basically

249
00:10:33,030 --> 00:10:34,880
just a more turn-key
way of doing

250
00:10:34,880 --> 00:10:37,540
unit based testing where
you can install Ceedling,

251
00:10:37,540 --> 00:10:39,420
create a new Ceedling project,

252
00:10:39,420 --> 00:10:42,010
add any of your project files
in the source directory,

253
00:10:42,010 --> 00:10:44,750
and then use the test files
in a test directory with

254
00:10:44,750 --> 00:10:46,640
this specific pattern using

255
00:10:46,640 --> 00:10:48,470
test filename and the tests

256
00:10:48,470 --> 00:10:50,300
like we just discussed before.

257
00:10:50,300 --> 00:10:53,660
Then just run Ceedling
tests:all and that will go out,

258
00:10:53,660 --> 00:10:55,010
build all the runners for you,

259
00:10:55,010 --> 00:10:57,300
auto detect all your
automated tests,

260
00:10:57,300 --> 00:11:00,360
and update, and run every
single time you call it.

261
00:11:00,360 --> 00:11:02,050
This is a great tool to use with

262
00:11:02,050 --> 00:11:05,825
automation to automatically
pull in new tests

263
00:11:05,825 --> 00:11:08,430
based on your source
code and verify that all

264
00:11:08,430 --> 00:11:09,760
of your unit tests are passing

265
00:11:09,760 --> 00:11:11,190
as a part of an
automated build test.

266
00:11:11,190 --> 00:11:16,710
A second unit test framework

267
00:11:16,710 --> 00:11:17,920
that I wanted to
mention I've used in

268
00:11:17,920 --> 00:11:19,475
the past is GoogleTest.

269
00:11:19,475 --> 00:11:23,115
This one is a little
more C++ centric,

270
00:11:23,115 --> 00:11:26,090
so you have a project
that's already using C++,

271
00:11:26,090 --> 00:11:27,615
it might be a good
one to consider,

272
00:11:27,615 --> 00:11:29,930
whereas Unity kit is pure C

273
00:11:29,930 --> 00:11:32,705
and can be used on
pure C projects,

274
00:11:32,705 --> 00:11:34,710
GoogleTest to C++ centric,

275
00:11:34,710 --> 00:11:37,755
but it can also be
used to test C files.

276
00:11:37,755 --> 00:11:41,110
It uses C++ streams
for little bit

277
00:11:41,110 --> 00:11:44,495
more verbose exception handling
and for error handling.

278
00:11:44,495 --> 00:11:48,130
In my opinion, that's a little
bit easier to enumerate

279
00:11:48,130 --> 00:11:51,220
errors and make
more verbose errors

280
00:11:51,220 --> 00:11:53,275
talking about what
the failures are.

281
00:11:53,275 --> 00:11:57,005
It has a similar google mock
framework for C++ mocks.

282
00:11:57,005 --> 00:12:00,780
It's not maybe quite as
automated as Unity is,

283
00:12:00,780 --> 00:12:03,650
but it's still very functional.

284
00:12:03,650 --> 00:12:06,610
It's used on OpenCV
and ROS code bases,

285
00:12:06,610 --> 00:12:08,040
so it's pretty popular,

286
00:12:08,040 --> 00:12:10,110
and it's widely used,

287
00:12:10,110 --> 00:12:12,550
likely to be around for awhile.